.TH OMATRACK 1 "October 2026" "omatrack 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
omatrack \- terminal tracker for .mod, .xm, and .it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B omatrack
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile\fR]
.br
.B omatrack \-\-render
.I out.wav
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I file
.br
.B omatrack \-\-help
.br
.B omatrack \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omatrack
is a terminal tracker for 31-sample, 4-channel ProTracker modules
.RB ( M.K. ,
.BR M!K! ,
.BR FLT4 ,
and
.BR 4CHN ),
and a player for FastTracker 2
.B .xm
and Impulse Tracker
.B .it
files.
Format is detected from the header
.RB ( "Extended Module: "
or
.BR IMPM ),
not only from the extension.
XM and IT songs are shown, played, edited, and saved, including more than four channels.
.B Ctrl-S
writes the format of the open file.
Save As uses the extension
.RB ( .mod ,
.BR .xm ,
or
.BR .it ).
A conversion that cannot be stored exactly is described on the status line.
The open song is left unchanged when the extension is missing or the file cannot be written.
With no file it reopens the last module, including
.B .xm
and
.BR .it ,
or starts an empty module when there is none
or
.B \-\-no\-reopen
is set.
A missing or invalid remembered file starts empty and says why.
.PP
The view needs about 76 columns and 20 rows.
Space plays from the cursor.
Enter toggles edit mode.
A
.B *
after the title means the song has unsaved edits.
Quit, new, and open ask before discarding them.
.PP
Colors come from the active Omarchy theme when one is installed, otherwise
from the built-in ProTracker palette.
See
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-theme " \fIname\fR"
.BR auto ,
.BR omarchy ,
.BR protracker ,
.BR phosphor ,
or
.BR terminal .
Overrides the config file.
.B terminal
uses ANSI colors so the terminal's own palette shows through.
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Read this config file instead of the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reopen
Do not load the last module on this launch.
A file argument is still opened, and still becomes the remembered path.
.TP
.BR \-\-render " \fIout.wav\fR"
Mix the song to 16-bit stereo WAV and exit.
Does not open an audio device.
.TP
.BR \-\-rate " \fIhz\fR"
Sample rate for
.BR \-\-render .
Default is the config value, or 44100.
.TP
.BR \-\-max-seconds " \fIn\fR"
Stop
.B \-\-render
after
.I n
seconds if the song does not loop.
Default is the config value, or 600.
.TP
.BR \-\-interpolate " \fImode\fR"
.B linear
or
.BR nearest .
.TP
.BR \-\-separation " \fI0\-100\fR"
100 is hard Amiga panning.
0 is mono.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the key list and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Enter
Toggle edit mode.
.TP
.B Space
Play or stop.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
Rewind to order 0, row 0.
If the song is playing, playback continues from the start and channel
voices, pattern loops, and meters are cleared.
If the song is stopped, the cursor moves to the start and playback
stays stopped.
.B r
is not this key.
In edit mode it enters a note, and on the sample list it renames
the instrument.
Text entry and the file menu ignore
.BR Ctrl-R .
.TP
.B ?
Key list.
.B Esc
closes it.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F
File menu:
.B n
new,
.B o
open,
.B s
save,
.B a
save as.
.TP
.B Ctrl-S
Save in the format of the open file.
An
.B .xm
stays XM, an
.B .it
stays IT, and a
.B .mod
stays a module.
XM and IT write every pattern, including ones the order list does not reference.
A
.B .mod
still stores only patterns through the highest order entry.
An untitled module asks for a path.
Save As
.RB ( Ctrl-F ,
then
.BR a )
picks
.BR .mod ,
.BR .xm ,
or
.B .it
from the extension.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Z / Ctrl-Y
Undo / redo.
.TP
.B q, Esc, Ctrl-Q
Quit.
.B q
works from browse mode.
.B Ctrl-Q
works anywhere.
.B Esc
clears a block, then leaves edit mode, then quits.
Each asks when the song is modified.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Copy.
It does not quit.
.TP
.B Tab / Shift-Tab
Next or previous pane.
In edit mode, Tab changes channel.
.TP
.B 1 2 3 4
Mute a channel.
In edit mode use
.BR Alt-1 " through " Alt-4 .
.TP
.B F1 / F2
Octave, 1 through 3.
.TP
.B F3 / F4
Edit step, 0 through 16.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Left / Ctrl-Right
While the pattern pane is focused, in browse or edit, change which pattern
is shown.
They do not change the order list.
The pattern pane title is
.B Pattern 15
and, when that is not the pattern stored in the current order slot,
.BR "Pattern 15 (slot 03 = 07)" .
In edit mode the title is
.B EDIT 15
with the same slot note.
.B Ctrl-Right
past the last pattern appends one blank pattern and shows it.
That pattern is not inserted into the order.
.B Ctrl-Left
on pattern 0 does nothing.
.B ,
and
.B .
change the viewed pattern the same way and stop at the ends.
.B [
and
.B ]
still move between order positions.
Up and Down in the song pane change the pattern number in the current
order slot, and every existing pattern stays selectable, including one
the order list does not reference.
Changing a slot does not delete pattern data.
Undo and redo restore the slot or an appended pattern.
Playback follows the order list, not the pattern on screen.
.B Ctrl-Right
past the last pattern appends one blank pattern for
.BR .mod ,
.BR .xm ,
and
.BR .it .
The bottom row lists shortcuts for the focused pane and drops the ones
that do not fit.
A
.B .mod
stores patterns through the highest order entry.
A pattern above that stays in memory, and the status line says that
reopening the file will not have it, until an order slot references it.
.TP
.B F5
Cycle the visualization: the spectrum beside the sample list, then the
scope in that same pane, then hidden, then the spectrum again.
Channel meters stay under the pattern columns in every mode and scroll
with those columns.
The tracker opens on the spectrum unless
.B default_view
says otherwise.
.B off
lets the sample list use the full width.
.PP
In edit mode the lower piano row is
.B Z S X D C V G B H N J M
and the upper row is
.BR "Q 2 W 3 E R 5 T 6 Y 7 U" .
.B ?
inside the program lists block, order, and sample keys.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The default file is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/omatrack/config.toml ,
or
.I ~/.config/omatrack/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
A missing file uses the defaults.
A file that cannot be parsed is ignored, and the reason is shown on the
status line.
An example is installed at
.IR /usr/share/omatrack/config.toml .
.PP
.EX
reopen_last = true
default_view = "spectrum"
theme = "auto"
[audio]
sample_rate = 44100
interpolation = "linear"
stereo_separation = 100
max_seconds = 600
[edit]
octave = 2
step = 1
.EE
.PP
.B reopen_last
controls startup only.
The path itself is stored under
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/omatrack/last_file
or
.IR ~/.local/state/omatrack/last_file ,
not in this file.
It is updated on open, save, and save as, and cleared for a new untitled module.
.B default_view
is
.BR spectrum ,
.BR scope ,
or
.BR off .
.B spectrum
is the default.
The spectrum and the scope share the right half of the sample row,
not a panel under the pattern.
.SH THEMES
.B auto
reads the active Omarchy theme.
Current Omarchy writes it to
.I ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme
.RB ( $XDG_STATE_HOME
when that is set).
Older Omarchy used
.IR ~/.config/omarchy/current/theme .
The canonical palette is
.IR colors.toml .
If that file is absent, omatrack reads
.IR alacritty.toml ,
.IR kitty.conf ,
or
.I ghostty.conf
from the same directory.
.PP
.B SIGUSR1
reloads the theme, and so does a change to those files.
Truecolor is used when
.B COLORTERM
is
.I truecolor
or
.IR 24bit ,
or when
.B TERM
names kitty, alacritty, or ghostty.
Otherwise colors are quantized to 256 or 16 ANSI colors.
.B \-\-theme terminal
skips that and uses the terminal palette directly.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/omatrack/config.toml
User configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/omatrack/last_file
Last opened or saved module.
Not edited by hand; see
.B reopen_last
above.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme/
Active Omarchy theme on current installs.
.TP
.I ~/.config/omarchy/current/theme/
Active Omarchy theme on older installs.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, or help or version was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The module, the WAV file, or the terminal could not be used.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was not understood.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
The in-program help
.RB ( ? )
and the project README.
